1. Recommended Setup (Before Installation)

✅ Use a separate EC2 instance
❌ Do NOT install SonarQube together with Nexus, Git, Maven, etc. on the same small server.

SonarQube is memory hungry.

Minimum Requirements:

t3.medium or higher

4GB+ RAM

20GB+ storage

🛠 2. Install Prerequisites

Connect to your EC2:

ssh -i your-key.pem ec2-user@your-ec2-ip

Update system:

sudo dnf update -y
✅ Install Java 17 (Required for SonarQube 10+)

Check if Java exists:

java -version

If not installed:

sudo dnf install java-17-amazon-corretto -y

Verify:

java -version
✅ Install PostgreSQL (Recommended Database)

SonarQube DOES NOT recommend H2 for production.

Install PostgreSQL:

sudo dnf install postgresql15-server postgresql15 -y

Initialize DB:

sudo postgresql-setup --initdb

Start PostgreSQL:

sudo systemctl enable postgresql
sudo systemctl start postgresql

Check status:

sudo systemctl status postgresql
🗄 3. Configure Database for SonarQube

Switch to postgres user:

sudo -i -u postgres

Open psql:

psql

Create database:

CREATE DATABASE sonarqube;
CREATE USER sonar WITH ENCRYPTED PASSWORD 'sonarpassword';
GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON DATABASE sonarqube TO sonar;
\q

Exit:

exit
👤 4. Create Sonar User (Best Practice)

Create system user:

sudo useradd sonar
📥 5. Download & Install SonarQube

Go to official site and copy latest LTS version link.

Example:

cd /opt
sudo dnf install wget unzip -y
sudo wget https://binaries.sonarsource.com/Distribution/sonarqube/sonarqube-10.4.0.XXXX.zip

Unzip:

sudo unzip sonarqube-*.zip

Rename:

sudo mv sonarqube-* sonarqube

Set permissions:

sudo chown -R sonar:sonar /opt/sonarqube
⚙ 6. Configure SonarQube Database Connection

Edit config:

sudo nano /opt/sonarqube/conf/sonar.properties

Find and edit:

sonar.jdbc.username=sonar
sonar.jdbc.password=sonarpassword
sonar.jdbc.url=jdbc:postgresql://localhost/sonarqube

Save & exit.

🔐 7. Increase System Limits (Important)

Edit:

sudo nano /etc/sysctl.conf

Add:

vm.max_map_count=262144
fs.file-max=65536

Apply:

sudo sysctl -p

Edit limits:

sudo nano /etc/security/limits.conf

Add:

sonar   -   nofile   65536
sonar   -   nproc    4096
🚀 8. Create Systemd Service (Auto Start)

Create service:

sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/sonarqube.service

Paste:

[Unit]
Description=SonarQube Service
After=network.target

[Service]
Type=forking
User=sonar
Group=sonar
ExecStart=/opt/sonarqube/bin/linux-x86-64/sonar.sh start
ExecStop=/opt/sonarqube/bin/linux-x86-64/sonar.sh stop
Restart=always

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target

Reload daemon:

sudo systemctl daemon-reload

Enable service:

sudo systemctl enable sonarqube

Start:

sudo systemctl start sonarqube

Check status:

sudo systemctl status sonarqube
🌍 9. Open Firewall Port

Default SonarQube port:

9000

If using AWS Security Group:

👉 Add inbound rule:

Type: Custom TCP

Port: 9000

Source: Your IP or 0.0.0.0/0 (for testing)

🔎 10. Access SonarQube

Open browser:

http://your-ec2-public-ip:9000

Default login:

Username: admin
Password: admin

You will be forced to change password.

✅ After Installation (Important For DevOps Practice)

You can now:

✔ Integrate with Maven
✔ Integrate with Jenkins
✔ Integrate with GitHub Actions
✔ Scan Java project

Example Maven scan:

mvn sonar:sonar \
  -Dsonar.projectKey=my-project \
  -Dsonar.host.url=http://your-ip:9000 \
  -Dsonar.login=your-token
🚨 Important Advice For You (Based On Your Previous Server Issues)

You mentioned:

my server refuse to load after installing many tools

✔ Good decision to use separate servers
✔ SonarQube + Nexus + Jenkins should NOT be installed together on small EC2

Best architecture:

EC2-1 → Jenkins
EC2-2 → SonarQube
EC2-3 → Nexus

That is production-like setup.
